const title = "LG Dryer Not Heating? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "LG dryer runs but won't heat? Usually a clogged vent (a fire risk), then the thermal fuse, heating element, or thermistor. Where the d80 Flow Sense warning fits, and when it's a tech job.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my LG dryer not heating?",
    a: "On an LG dryer that tumbles but won't heat, the first thing to rule out is a restricted exhaust vent — a clogged vent makes the dryer overheat and blows its thermal fuse, which is the single most common LG no-heat part. After the vent and fuse, the usual failed parts are the heating element and the thermistor (temperature sensor). On a gas LG, a weak igniter, a gas-valve coil, or a bad flame sensor stops the burner from lighting. If your LG is showing a d80, d90, or d95 warning instead, that's the Flow Sense airflow system telling you the vent is restricted — clean it and the code clears (see our dedicated LG d80/d90/d95 guide)."
  },
  {
    q: "My LG dryer shows d80/d90/d95 — is that why it's not heating?",
    a: "Those aren't heating faults, they're LG's Flow Sense airflow warnings — d80 means roughly 80% vent blockage, rising to d90 and d95, at which point the dryer cools down and effectively stops drying to protect itself. So yes, a badly restricted vent behind a d95 will leave your clothes cold. The fix is cleaning the filter and the full exhaust vent, and the code clears on its own. We keep the full walkthrough on a separate page — see the LG d80/d90/d95 guide linked below — so this page focuses on the no-heat causes that aren't airflow."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix an LG dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe, high-value step is yours: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent, including the hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap. That resolves most no-heat and Flow Sense calls and removes a real fire hazard. Also reset both halves of the 240V breaker on an electric LG — if one leg trips, the drum tumbles but the heat is dead. What you should not do is open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, heating element, or thermistor, and never touch the gas line or gas valve on a gas LG. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire hazard?",
    a: "The clogged vent that most often stops an LG from heating is also a leading fire cause. The U.S. Fire Administration reports roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. That's why an LG running hot, throwing a Flow Sense warning, or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day — it fixes the heat and removes the hazard together."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing an LG dryer that won't heat?",
    a: "Usually yes. The LG no-heat parts — thermal fuse, heating element, thermistor on an electric model, or igniter, gas-valve coils, and flame sensor on a gas model — are among the more affordable dryer repairs. Our techs verify the vent first (a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again if you don't clear it), then test the heating circuit and replace the failed part. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

