<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/lg-not-heating"
  eyebrow="LG Dryer Not Heating · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Dryer Runs but Won't Heat? Check the Vent and the Fuse"
  subtitle="Most LG no-heat calls trace to a clogged vent and a blown thermal fuse — and that vent is a fire risk. Here's what to check, where the d80 Flow Sense warning fits, and when it's the element or thermistor."
  heroImageTitle="LG Dryer Not Heating Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">An LG that won't heat is usually a choked vent and a blown fuse.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an LG dryer tumbles but won't heat, start with the exhaust vent. A clogged vent makes the dryer overheat and blows its thermal fuse — the single most common LG no-heat part. After that come the heating element and the thermistor. On a gas LG, it's a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or the flame sensor. If instead you're seeing a d80, d90, or d95, that's LG's Flow Sense airflow warning (covered on its own guide) — same root cause, a restricted vent. Clean the filter and full vent first: it fixes most no-heat calls and removes a genuine fire hazard. Still cold on a clear vent? It's a diagnostic. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your LG from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. A dryer running hot or showing a Flow Sense warning is worth clearing the same day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 CAUSES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Why an LG dryer stops heating</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Restricted vent → blown thermal fuse (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes the airflow, the dryer overheats, and LG's <strong>thermal fuse</strong> — a one-time safety device that doesn't reset — blows and cuts the heat. It's the number-one LG no-heat part, and it only stays fixed once the vent that blew it is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>The element heats the air on an electric LG. When it burns open, the dryer tumbles with no heat. Tested for continuity and replaced inside the cabinet — a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermistor (temperature sensor)</h3>
      <p>The thermistor reports the air temperature to the control; a failing one can cut heat or, on some models, show a tE-type sensor code. It's tested against spec and replaced.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Flow Sense d80 / d90 / d95 — airflow, not a heat part</h3>
      <p>These are airflow-restriction warnings, not heating failures: at d95 the dryer cools and effectively stops. They point back to the vent. We keep the full walkthrough on the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-d80-error/">LG d80/d90/d95 guide</a> rather than repeating it here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas LG, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve solenoid coils, or a bad flame sensor. The tell is an igniter that glows then goes out without the gas lighting. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the flexible hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat and Flow Sense calls — and removes the fire hazard.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-cycle a stuck warning.</strong> After cleaning the vent, unplug for a minute and restart to clear a lingering Flow Sense code.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix a large share of LG no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, heating element, or thermistor, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? Time for a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the LG still won't heat, the fault is inside: an open thermal fuse (plus the vent that blew it), a burned-out heating element, a failing thermistor, or on gas the igniter, valve coils, or flame sensor. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace only the failed part. These are among the more affordable dryer repairs, so a no-heat LG is usually well worth fixing. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide (all brands), the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference, and the dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-d80-error/">LG d80/d90/d95</a> Flow Sense guide. If your LG takes multiple cycles rather than none, that's <a href="/services/dryer-repair/takes-too-long/">dryer takes too long</a> — usually the same vent. A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-dryer-repair/">LG dryer repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

